# Code Casting Rules

When writing implementation code:

- You are filling a mold within the SDK constraint surface. The tests define the shape. The SDK defines the vocabulary.
- Read the specification, SDK interfaces, and tests before writing code.
- Implement SDK interfaces. Do not introduce abstractions outside the SDK.
- Stay within the assigned module boundary.
- Use interface contracts as defined. Do not modify them or SDK interfaces.
- If a test seems wrong, report it. Do not change it.
- If you need a building block not in the SDK, stop and request SDK extension.
- Make small, atomic commits toward passing tests.
- Do not add behavior not required by the specification.
- Do not introduce undocumented dependencies.
- Prefer clarity over cleverness.
- For user-facing code, all visual values (colors, fonts, sizes, spacing, shadows, radii) must reference design tokens from the SDK. Zero hardcoded hex, px, rem, or font-family values.
- When implementing UI, reference the design identity document for aesthetic direction, permitted patterns, and banned patterns. If no design identity exists for user-facing work, stop and report the gap.
- Do not use known AI-default patterns when the design identity document specifies alternatives (e.g. Inter/Roboto/Arial as primary typeface, framework-default palettes, purple-blue gradient heroes, generic copy cataloged in the design identity).
- For user-facing code, ensure all interactive elements are keyboard-accessible, all images have appropriate alt text, all form inputs have labels, and color contrast meets the design identity's WCAG target. Run automated accessibility checks (axe-core or equivalent) alongside linting. Accessibility failures are casting defects.
- When all assigned tests pass, stop.

See `agent/CODE_CASTING_RULES.md` for detailed guidance.
